    {
      "segment_id": 2,
      "source_block_ids": [
        31
      ],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "paragraph",
        "verbatim_text": "**Example 2:** If B and Q are acute angles such that $\\sin B = \\sin Q$ , then prove that $\\angle B = \\angle Q$ ."
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "show",
        "visual_layer": "hide",
        "avatar_layer": "show",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    },
    {
      "segment_id": 3,
      "source_block_ids": [
        31
      ],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "paragraph",
        "verbatim_text": "We start with two right-angled triangles, one containing angle B and the other containing angle Q. We are given that the sine of B is equal to the sine of Q."
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "hide",
        "visual_layer": "show",
        "avatar_layer": "gesture_only",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    },
    {
      "segment_id": 4,
      "source_block_ids": [
        32,
        33,
        34
      ],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "formula",
        "formulas": [
          "$$\\sin B = \\sin Q$$",
          "$$\\Rightarrow \\frac{AC}{AB} = \\frac{PR}{PQ}$$",
          "$$\\Rightarrow \\frac{AC}{PR} = \\frac{AB}{PQ} = k \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$"
        ]
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "hide",
        "visual_layer": "show",
        "avatar_layer": "gesture_only",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    },
    {
      "segment_id": 5,
      "source_block_ids": [
        35,
        36,
        37,
        38,
        39
      ],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "formula",
        "formulas": [
          "$$BC = \\sqrt{AB^2 - AC^2} \\text{ [By Pythagoras theorem]}$$",
          "$$\\Rightarrow \\sqrt{k^2 PQ^2 - k^2 PR^2}$$",
          "$$\\Rightarrow k \\sqrt{PQ^2 - PR^2} \\text{ [from (1)]}$$",
          "$$QR = \\sqrt{PQ^2 - PR^2}$$",
          "$$\\Rightarrow \\frac{BC}{QR} = \\frac{k \\sqrt{PQ^2 - PR^2}}{\\sqrt{PQ^2 - PR^2}} = k \\quad \\text{--- (2)}$$"
        ]
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "hide",
        "visual_layer": "show",
        "avatar_layer": "gesture_only",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    },
    {
      "segment_id": 6,
      "source_block_ids": [
        40
      ],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "paragraph",
        "verbatim_text": "From (1) and (2), $\\frac{AC}{PR} = \\frac{AB}{PQ} = \\frac{BC}{QR} = k$. Since all three corresponding sides are in the same proportion, the two triangles are similar. Therefore, $\\angle B = \\angle Q$."
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "hide",
        "visual_layer": "show",
        "avatar_layer": "gesture_only",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    },
    {
      "segment_id": 7,
      "source_block_ids": [],
      "visual_content": {
        "content_type": "paragraph",
        "verbatim_text": "Combining trigonometric ratios with geometric principles like similar triangles allows us to establish fundamental angle relationships."
      },
      "display_directives": {
        "text_layer": "show",
        "visual_layer": "hide",
        "avatar_layer": "show",
        "flip_timing_sec": null
      }
    }
  ]
